[Seif 1] “Be strong as a lion… that he awaken the dawn.” Shelah writes that there is a sod (mystical reason) to join day and night with Torah or prayer, morning and evening. See Magen Avraham subparagraph 1, Eliyah Rabbah 1, Kitzur Shulchan Aruch 1, Razah 8.<br>
<br>
There, in the Rama’s gloss: “I have set Hashem before me always”—one should picture the four-letter Name always before one’s eyes; this greatly benefits yiras Shamayim and purifies the soul. Shaar Ruach HaKodesh, Yichud 21.<br>
<br>
There, in the Rama’s gloss: “Nor be ashamed before people,” yet in any case he should not quarrel with them. Beis Yosef; Magen Avraham subparagraph 2.<br>
<br>
There, in the Rama’s gloss: “Immediately when he awakens,” etc.—not literally; pause a little and do not stand up suddenly right after sleep (Magen Avraham subparagraph 3). Eliyah Rabbah there. Seder HaYom writes that on rising one says Modeh ani… shehechezarta bi nishmasi b’chemlah rabbah emunasach. Netilas yadayim is not required for this even if the hands are soiled, because one does not mention the Name or a kinui. Shulchan Aruch 17:4; Chayei Adam, general rule 1 end of os 1; Razah 5; Kitzur Shulchan Aruch 1:2. Although Chida in Kesher Godel 1:1 writes that some require netilas yadayim for Modeh ani, since most poskim hold it is unnecessary, we follow them.<br>
<br>
One may recite this formula whether still lying down or sitting. Razah there. Pause slightly between the words b’chemlah and rabbah (Kitzur Shulchan Aruch there). But to utter words of Torah with unwashed hands is forbidden. Olas Tamid; Shulchan Aruch there; Razah there. See below, siman 4, os 110.<br>
<br>
As soon as he awakens from sleep he should wash his hands properly if he does not intend to sleep again even while still lying in bed. Eliyah Rabbah 4, citing Shelah; Olas Tamid; likewise Be’er Heitev 1 in the name of Derech Chayim.
